1. THE BOOK THE LIMITS WOULD HAVE GOVERNED -- equal weight, real panel

    return 17.18%/yr, volatility 18.52%, Sharpe 0.928
    WORST DRAWDOWN            -45.2%
    declared max_drawdown_pct 15.0%   -> BREACHED
    first breach on day 508 of 6,800; the book spends 8.2% of all days below it
    declared max_daily_loss_pct 3.0% would have fired 72 times in 27 years

2. HOW DEEP A HEALTHY BOOK GOES, with this panel's real tails

    excess kurtosis of the real series: 9.5 (0 for normal), so normal draws would understate this

    book                             median     95th     99th    breach 15%
    -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    the goal: Sharpe 2.0 at 10% vol  -17.0%   -24.5%   -28.6%          76%
    Sharpe 1.5 at 10% vol           -18.3%   -27.3%   -32.1%          84%
    the equal-weight book           -37.0%   -53.9%   -61.6%         100%

    A limit is a 'something broke' signal only if a HEALTHY book rarely reaches it.
    For the goal that means at least 24% (reached by 1 path in 20) and 29% (1 in 100).
    Where between them is risk appetite and belongs to the owner. 15% is not
    an appetite: it fires on 76% of healthy histories.

3. DO THE DECLARED NUMBERS AGREE WITH EACH OTHER AND WITH THE GOAL

    max_total_risk_pct 30% caps invested capital, while max_leverage 2.0 permits 200%.
    The leverage limit allows 6.7x what the total limit allows, so it can never bind: it is decoration.

    A 20% return on TOTAL capital with only 30% invested needs 66.7% on the invested part.
    The equal-weight book makes 17.18% and SPY makes 10.80% (R48), so that is about 3.9x the market.
    The goal and the total-risk cap cannot both be right.

    max_position_size_pct 10% against the 30% cap means at least 3 positions. R33 measured 110 names
    as 3.16 independent bets, so that is roughly all the independence on offer -- the one
    pair of these numbers that agrees with a measurement.
